## O3. chromium-mp4-capture — hardware encode + single-generation exports  [P1, two branches, (a) before (b)]
Why: Chromium channel recording is webm (VP8/VP9 = SOFTWARE encode, ×2 streams) while the composite
runs a third (hardware) encode — the biggest capture CPU burn. And unedited exports ship the composite,
i.e. a SECOND lossy generation of an already-encoded source.
(a) ee/o3-mp4-recorder: Chromium MediaRecorder MIME preference gains mp4(h264+aac) FIRST when
    isTypeSupported (webm stays in the list as fallback — additive); track.contentHint='text' (screen)
    / 'motion' (camera) at acquire; camera requests 1920×1080 ideal when the config has no screen
    channel (camera-full takes currently upscale 720p — a real, visible defect).
(b) ee/o3-single-gen: a take with exactly ONE video channel whose dims == export dims and codec is
    mp4-compatible h264 → skip the live composite entirely (audio mix graph still runs); unedited
    export = packet-copy the RAW channel (extend instant.ts source selection). Composite continues for
    multi-video and letterboxed cases.
GATES (a): oracle sync 10/10 in band · capture CPU before/after on the 08-22 rig (report %, expect a
large drop, software VP→hardware h264) · Safari spoofed-UA smoke unchanged · salvage smoke green.
GATES (b): screen-only 1080p take: console proves no composite recorder · instant export ≤ current ·
PSNR/visual evidence that single-generation ≥ composite-generation quality · capture write bandwidth
~halved (bytes/s logged) · all oracles green.

## O4. webcodecs-engine — the keystone  [P1, big; CONTINUES ee/webcodecs-capture-2 (on origin, behind main — rebase first)]
Why: the pixel path (MediaRecorder + <video> + main-thread 2D canvas) forces 4–6 redundant decodes
during capture, fixed-tick redraws of static frames, no keyframe control, ±45 ms epoch jitter, and the
1080p capture ceiling.
Step 1 (gate to proceed): root-cause the PROVEN +15–25 ms cross-architecture systematic blocking the
≤10 ms sync gate. Per STATE it is NOT video-side — audit the audio anchor (min-filter and catch-up
rejection in measuredAudio.ts) and the oracle's flash model. Fix instrument or anchor; prove ×10 runs.
Step 2: MediaStreamTrackProcessor → VideoFrames transferred to a WORKER compositor (OffscreenCanvas;
WebGPU importExternalTexture preferred, WebGL then 2D fallback) → VideoEncoder (prefer-hardware,
realtime, keyframe cadence ≤2 s — the smart-cut prerequisite) → fMP4 mux → positioned durable OPFS
writer. Frame-driven draw (encode on frame arrival, ~1 fps keep-alive when static), not a fixed 30 Hz
tick. The on-screen recording preview renders the compositor's own output — zero extra <video>
decodes. SourceLiveness feeds from frame arrivals (worklet tick remains the clock). Raw channel
capture may also move to VideoEncoder on Chromium in the same worker — optional, evidence decides.
Constraints: MediaRecorder path stays as capability/watchdog fallback (weak machine degrades, proven);
crash-salvage must recover the fMP4 composite mid-write (ABSORBS v1 task 4 — composite salvage becomes
free); measured-audio pipeline untouched; instant export contract unchanged.
GATES: oracle sync ≤10 ms 10/10 · 08-22 4K rig: delivered ≥28 fps at 1080p output (baseline 21.5/11.9)
· mid-record tab-kill salvages composite + all channels · forced-slow harness proves watchdog fallback
· capture CPU ≤ O3 baseline (report) · unedited instant export ≤ 200 ms · ZERO TAIL LOSS at stop
(distinctive final-second event present in export, drain-verified — PO hard requirement) · all oracles green.

## O5. export-engine-v2 — worker, pipelined, smart-cut, codec ladder  [P1, after O4]
Why: the render is main-thread and strictly serial (decode awaits draw awaits encode), sleeps every
8 frames, tops out near realtime. Hardware decode+encode overlapped → multi-× realtime.
Goal: (a) exportRecording moves to a dedicated worker (already OffscreenCanvas-based; progress via
postMessage); yield hacks deleted. (b) stage pipelining with small ring buffers + encodeQueueSize
backpressure (decode-ahead 4–8 frames per source). (c) SMART-CUT: packet-copy full GOPs between cut
points, re-encode only boundary GOPs (needs O4 keyframe cadence; full render remains for old takes).
Generalize to DIRTY-SEGMENT rendering: only spans whose PIXELS an edit changed re-encode (zoom/camera
move during minute 2 → render minute 2, packet-copy the rest) — this is what keeps even visual edits
near-instant; F2/F4 tracks must expose per-span dirty ranges for it.
(d) codec ladder above avc: probe hevc/av1 hardware encode (isConfigSupported, mp4 container); avc
floor, vp9/webm last (codecs.ts FALLBACK_CHAINS is the seam). (e) VFR option (PO-approved): preserve
source presentation timestamps from frame-driven takes instead of CFR resampling — high-Hz scrolls
stay smooth; CFR 30 stays the default.
Constraints: certified audio path behavior identical (fidelity oracle is the proof); progress UI keeps
working; abort works mid-pipeline.
GATES: 60 s 1080p30 edited synthetic take ≥5× realtime on the TD machine (report ×; stretch 8×) ·
trim-only edit of a 30 s take ≤1 s · zero main-thread long tasks >50 ms during export (trace) · every
ladder rung's file plays (Chrome + Safari where the codec applies) · fidelity+sync green.

